WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.368
-Welcome to the show!
I've been looking forward

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.369
to this for a very long time.
-Oh,

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:04.437
well, thanks for having me.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.437
It's a treat!

00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:06.906
-So you win a Pulitzer

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.074
for "All the Light
We Cannot See,"

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.843
which is a book
that I loved a great deal.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:13.145
It was nice to find out that
other people loved it as well.

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:15.715
So I'm worried, though --
Not worried,

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.550
but I guess I'm curious --

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.118
when you're writing
a follow-up book,

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:20.853
how much is that
in the back of your head,

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:22.555
that the last one
won the Pulitzer?

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.623
-Oh.
When you're writing,

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:26.292
it's not as much in your head,
but it's in your head

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.928
at other moments.
Like you're on the airplane

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:32.665
and you can't finish the easy
level Sudoku and you're like,

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:36.002
"Pulitzer Prize Winner Fails
Again at the Easy Sudoku."

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.438
[ Laughter ]
You know, there's moments

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:39.939
the imposter syndrome comes out.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.809
-Sure.
-Thankfully, writing is
so engaging to me that,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.977
five minutes into your workday,
you're just trying to solve

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.312
the problems
of what you're writing

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.914
and that whole world
kind of strips away.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:50.883
But there's moments you're doing
the dishes or something

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:53.119
and you're like, "Oh, wait.
Am I supposed to do this again?"

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.687
-[ Laughs ]
-You know, I kind of feel like.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.855
-You're talking about like
sort of solving

00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:58.925
the problems of writing
and I think that there's always,

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.660
any writer, no matter
what they're writing,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.894
that's part of it.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.029
In "All the Light
We Cannot See,"

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.363
there were two perspectives.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:05.898
This book, it seems like

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.266
you made it more complicated
for yourself --

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:10.803
five perspectives,
three different eras.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:12.438
As I'm reading it,
I'm sort of picturing

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:13.706
how you had to map it out.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.441
Did you physically map it out?

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.642
-I did.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.711
Like a serial killer,
that kind of mapping.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.179
[ Laughter ]
It's crazy.

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:22.848
So, "All the Light" was kind of
an A/B, back-and-forth structure

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:25.618
between two characters,
Werner and Marie, A/B.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.620
Occasionally, I'd break that --
A/B, A/B --

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.121
ping-ponging back and forth.

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:30.590
But that was just enough

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.958
for me to push my brain
to hold that in.

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:35.861
And then, for this book,
which took me seven years,

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:39.098
I decided to go for five,
so like A/B/C/D/E.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.834
And I'm trying to keep
all five plates spinning

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.870
in a reader's mind
and you get anxious

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.272
that you'll forget what's
going on with character D,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.941
so you have
to go touch that plate

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.843
every once in a while,
to keep it spinning.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.043
So, often, at the beginning,

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.145
you don't really know
your structure perfectly,

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:55.348
so you're just drawing
these really rudimentary maps,

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:56.716
but, once you're years
into the project,

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.984
I'm drawing pretty
sophisticated maps,

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.788
trying to have like
all these little braids working

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:03.089
through the structure of it.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:05.658
Visual representation
helps me kind of figure out

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:07.093
where the story's going
sometimes.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.928
-And, because of that,
does that mean

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:13.699
you can't just write
character A from start to finish

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:14.933
and then move on
to the next one?

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:16.569
Do you have to then
sort of just, as a writer,

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:18.171
be going back and forth
between all of them?

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.072
-Yeah, yeah.
I will rarely write something

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.041
in the order in which
a reader will read it.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.642
You're often
disrupting chronology

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:25.177
just because you need to know
where you're going.

00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:27.312
It's just like when you leave
for somewhere,

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:28.647
you should know
your destination.

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:30.783
So, even though it's never
really a straight line,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.218
often, I'm writing the ending

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:36.622
long before I've built
all the connective tissue to it.

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.757
The great joy
of an ensemble novel like this

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:39.993
is there's always something
you work on.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.593
When you get stuck
on this character,

00:02:41.593 --> 00:02:43.495
you can go work
on a character --

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:44.997
I've got characters
in the 15th century.

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:46.766
I'll go work on the 15th century
for a week.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.768
And then,
if the paint starts to dry

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.302
on the contemporary stuff,
I'm like,

00:02:50.302 --> 00:02:52.438
"Oh, you know what? This week,
I gotta go work on those guys."

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.207
So it's kind of really joyful.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.209
There's always something
you can be working on

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.277
and, anytime you get stuck
in a place,

00:02:58.277 --> 00:02:59.745
you just find a new route.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:03.115
You can kind of figure out new
stuff to be happy working on.

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:06.385
-That is wonderful
and there is --

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:08.288
there are many joyful elements
to this book,

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.256
but it also sort of deals
with this, you know,

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.824
the idea
of a catastrophic future,

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.892
which it feels like so many
of us are drawn to,

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:16.595
when we choose our fiction,
not just in,

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:19.665
you know, literature,
but in television and films.

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:23.068
Was that something
that you wanted to focus on,

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.538
or did you just find that it was
the idea in your head

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:26.872
that you wanted
to put on the page?

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:28.241
-Yeah, great question.

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:30.076
Well, have you heard the phrase
Cloud Cuckoo Land before?

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.978
-I had never heard
Cloud Cuckoo Land.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:34.480
-I think a lot of folks have
and it's more present, I think,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.382
in British and Irish English,

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.818
but the phrase
way predates English.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:42.322
2400 years ago,
the Greek comic playwright,

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:45.191
a really hilarious writer
named Aristophanes,

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:46.392
wrote a play called "The Birds."

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.760
A bunch of his plays are gone.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.463
Dozens and dozens of plays
are lost, but seven survive.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:51.531
And this play called
"The Birds,"

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:53.733
which is the original
buddy comedy.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.536
It would be like Will Ferrell
would be in it now, basically.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:58.904
These two fools want
to leave Athens

00:03:58.904 --> 00:04:00.739
basically because
"there's too many lawyers"

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:02.741
and they build a new city
in the sky.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:06.145
It might be the first Western
utopian narrative written down.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.646
And that place is called
Cloud Cuckoo Land,

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:10.249
halfway between the realm
of the gods and the heavens,

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.518
and the place of the humans
on Earth.

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.254
And, that way, they can
intercept messages,
is what they plan.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:16.955
But, like all utopian
narratives, of course,

00:04:16.955 --> 00:04:19.191
it caves in, right?
Like Utopia's never

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.426
what they think
it's going to be.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:21.761
They start eating the birds

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.696
that help them
build the city and stuff.

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:26.632
And, of course, they fight
over power with each other.

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:29.268
And that phrase is really echoed
down through our language.

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:31.704
Now, it's sometimes used
in a disparaging way, like,

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:33.773
"Oh, Seth lives
in Cloud Cuckoo land.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.708
He thinks the virus
will be gone tomorrow,"

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:36.842
or something like that.

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:38.677
But it's also kind of
a beautiful thing.

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:39.979
I think it's really important

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:42.782
that we all dream
of better places, you know.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.851
So many human cultures
have told stories

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.519
about traveling on long journeys

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.620
to better places,
where there is no suffering.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.890
And so, as my kids, you know,
my kids were 10 to 17

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:53.225
while I was finishing this book

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:55.161
and, every time
I'd go downstairs,

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:58.164
there's like another dystopian
thing on TV.

00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:01.601
There's another city exploding
or another planet exploding.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:04.470
So I just thought, "What if I
explored utopian questions?"

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.739
Which, of course,
there's a great acreage

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:09.408
between that and dystopian
narratives, too.

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:10.610
What if I explored?
What does it mean

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.578
to dream of better places
in this book?

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.914
So, Cloud Cuckoo Land,
each of the five characters

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:18.618
kind of dreams of a better place
and works to reach that place,

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.286
but, ultimately, maybe, realizes

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:23.189
that home is going to be
the best that it gets

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:24.657
and kind of comes
to an acceptance

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.059
that maybe Cloud Cuckoo Land
is this place right now.

00:05:27.059 --> 00:05:28.994
This is as good
as it's going to get.

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:30.629
-That would be very nice,
if that

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.798
turned out to be the case.
-I hope so.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:35.368
-I also want to say,
which makes sense to me

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.503
because, when I read
"All the Light,"

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.872
it's very cinematic,
in a lot of ways,

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.641
and it's going to be
a television show,

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.242
which is so exciting.
-Yeah, thanks.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:44.276
It's exciting, yeah.

00:05:44.276 --> 00:05:46.546
Netflix is going to make
four episodes

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:47.780
and each is about one hour.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:48.881
I think I'm allowed to say that.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:49.982
And Steven Knight,

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.417
the writer who wrote
"Peaky Blinders,"

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.818
is writing the scripts.

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:55.655
He's really engaged and he's
so good at the crosscutting.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.423
I did a lot of crosscutting
in the novel,

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.525
all these short chapters
moving back and forth,

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.060
and he's really captured
that essence

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.661
in the scripts I've seen before.

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:05.698
And they're going to do a search
for the actor to play Marie.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.999
They're going to look
for a low-vision

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.267
or a blind actor to play her,
which is really special,

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:12.771
so I hope they find an amazing
young girl to play Marie.

00:06:12.771 --> 00:06:14.840
-It's so exciting.
I really can't thank you enough

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.442
for being here.
It's such a delight to meet you.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.444
-Same, Seth. Thanks.
-Thank you so much.

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.346
"Cloud Cuckoo Land" is
on sale now

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:22.080
wherever you buy your books,
but please remember

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.716
to support local
and independent bookstores.

